title: How to Learn (Study Skills)
subject: Study Skills
level: All
emoji: 🧠
description: The science of effective revision – use these on this very app!
---
What is active recall? :: Testing yourself from memory instead of re-reading – flashcards are perfect for this.
What is spaced repetition? :: Reviewing material at increasing intervals just as you're about to forget it.
What is the forgetting curve? :: Memory of new information drops rapidly unless it is reviewed (Ebbinghaus).
What is interleaving? :: Mixing different topics or problem types in one study session.
What is the Pomodoro Technique? :: Study for 25 minutes, then take a 5 minute break; take a longer break every four rounds.
What is the Feynman technique? :: Explain a concept in simple words as if teaching a beginner to find gaps in your understanding.
What is retrieval practice? :: Bringing information to mind from memory – it strengthens learning more than re-reading.
Why is sleep important for revision? :: Sleep helps consolidate memories, so revising before bed and sleeping well improves recall.
What is a mind map? :: A diagram that links ideas around a central topic using branches and keywords.
What is dual coding? :: Combining words with visuals (diagrams, drawings) to aid memory.
What is a mnemonic? :: A memory aid such as an acronym or rhyme (e.g. 'Richard Of York Gave Battle In Vain').
Why is cramming less effective than spacing out revision? :: Crammed knowledge fades quickly; spaced sessions build longer-lasting memory.
What should you do with cards you rate 'Again' in this app? :: Review them soon – they come back in the next session until you know them.
